        "intro": {
          "type": "editorial-rich-text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong>Short answer: you can use dark roast beans in a Jura, but only if they are dry to the touch.</strong> The issue is not the roast level itself - it is the oil. Oily beans coat the grinder burrs, clog the coffee chute, and gum up the brew unit. Over time, this leads to weak coffee, grinder jams, and expensive repairs.</p><p>This is the number one preventable maintenance issue on Jura machines, and the manual barely mentions it.</p>",
            "font_size": 16,
            "color": "#3f3029"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "heading_1": {
          "type": "editorial-heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Happens When You Use Oily Beans",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "show_rule": true,
            "color": "#25130d",
            "rule_color": "#ded7d1"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "content_1": {
          "type": "editorial-rich-text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>When beans have visible oil on their surface, that oil transfers to every surface it touches inside the machine:</p><ol><li><strong>Grinder burrs</strong> - oil coats the burrs, reducing their ability to grip and shatter beans consistently. Grind quality drops.</li><li><strong>Coffee chute</strong> - the narrow passage between grinder and brew unit collects oil residue. Over weeks, this builds up and narrows the chute, eventually blocking it.</li><li><strong>Brew unit</strong> - oil deposits on the piston, seals, and chamber walls. This produces rancid, stale flavors even with fresh beans.</li><li><strong>Bean hopper</strong> - oily residue accumulates on the hopper walls and the funnel opening, attracting old grounds and creating a sticky film.</li></ol><p>The damage is cumulative. You will not notice a problem after one bag. After 3-4 bags of oily beans, grind consistency drops. After 6+ bags, you may start getting weak coffee, grinder errors, or a complete chute blockage.</p>",
            "font_size": 16,
            "color": "#3f3029"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
